---
id: WC-001
type: rule
family: weak-cryptography
title: Deprecated hash primitive (MD5 / SHA-1) in a construction call
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 1

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs, .py, .java]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/test_*.py',
    '**/*_test.py',
    '**/tests/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: "createHash\\(\\s*['\"](?:md5|sha-?1)['\"]|hashlib\\.(?:md5|sha1)\\s*\\(|hashlib\\.new\\(\\s*['\"](?:md5|sha-?1)['\"]|MessageDigest\\.getInstance\\(\\s*['\"](?:md5|sha-?1)['\"]|subtle\\.digest\\(\\s*['\"](?:md5|sha-?1)['\"]|CryptoJS\\.(?:md5|sha1)\\b"
  flags: i

related: [WC-002]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

You're constructing a hash with a primitive that is broken for security
use. MD5 and SHA-1 have practical collision attacks — anything relying
on them for integrity, signatures, tokens, or password hashing can be
forged or substituted.

Switch to SHA-256 or better for integrity and signatures
(`createHash('sha256')`, `hashlib.sha256`,
`MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256")`, `subtle.digest('SHA-256', …)`). For
password storage use a slow salted KDF — argon2id, scrypt, or bcrypt — never
a bare hash. This rule matches the literal-algorithm construction forms
(`createHash('md5')`, `hashlib.md5(`, `hashlib.new('md5')`,
`MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA1")`, `subtle.digest('SHA-1', …)`); a hash
built from a *variable* algorithm name (`createHash(algo)`) is not visible to
a single-line regex and is not caught.

If this hash is a genuine *non-security* checksum (guarding against
accidental corruption, a cache key, dedup) and collision resistance does
not matter, keep it but say so: suppress WC-001 with a reason
(`# @anvil-ignore WC-001 -- non-security checksum for cache key`).
